Name: hyperTalkCompatible

Type: property

Syntax: set the hyperTalkCompatible of <stack> to {true | false}

Syntax: get the hyperTalkCompatible of <stack>

Summary:
Specifies whether a <stack> <import|imported> from <HyperCard> sends
its messages the way <HyperCard> did.

Associations: stack

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux, html5

Platforms: desktop, server

Example:
set the hyperTalkCompatible of stack "Address Book" to true

Example:
if the hyperTalkCompatible of this stack then answer "HyperCard rules"

Value (bool):
The <hyperTalkCompatible> of a <stack> is true or false. By default, it
is false.

Description:
Use the <hyperTalkCompatible> <property> to run a <stack> that came
from <HyperCard> with the rules its <script|scripts> were written for.
It changes nothing for a <stack> whose <hyperTalkCompatible> is false,
which is every <stack> unless a <script> sets it. File > Import Stack
sets it for each <stack> it imports, unless File > Import Stack >
HyperCard Message Order is turned off.

When it is true, eight things are as they were in <HyperCard>, for the
<stack> and for its substacks:

- A message that a <control> of a <background> group does not handle
  goes to the <card> before the <background>: a background button's
  <mouseUp> reaches the card's <script>, then the background's, then
  the stack's.
- A <handler> can be called <doMenu>.
- The <doMenu> <command> is first sent as a message to the <object>
  whose <handler> runs it, with the menu item as its parameter. The
  menu item is chosen only if no <handler> takes the message, or one
  passes it. The message is sent even while the <lockMessages> is
  true, as in <HyperCard>, whose lockMessages kept back only its own
  messages (<openCard>, <closeCard>...), and the <handler|handlers>
  run with it still true.
- The <lockRecent> keeps none of the <stack|stack's> <card|cards> out
  of the <recent cards|recent cards list>, so go back goes back through
  every <card> visited, as in <HyperCard>, whose lockRecent only kept
  <card|cards> out of the pictures of its Recent dialog. A <script>
  that sets the <lockRecent> to true, goes to a <card> and then goes
  back returns to the <card> it came from.
- A <handler> that goes to another <card>, with the <dynamicPaths>
  true, as File > Import Stack sets it, still sends its messages
  through its own <stack> first, as <HyperCard|HyperCard's> dynamic
  path did: a message goes from the <object> through its <card>,
  <background> and <stack>, then through the <card> the <handler> went
  to, that <card|card's> backgrounds and <stack>, and on to Home. No
  <object> gets the message twice, so a <background> the two
  <card|cards> share is left out, and so is a second trip through a
  <stack> the message has been through. Otherwise, a message that went
  to another <stack> goes on to the <card> there in place of the
  <handler|handler's> own <stack>, so a <handler> in the
  <stack|stack's> <script> is not found; and one that went to another
  <card> of its own <stack> reaches that <card> only in the
  <development environment>, where it goes through the <stack> a second
  time, and in a <standalone application> not at all.
- Arithmetic that gives no number goes on with <HyperCard|HyperCard's>
  result, an infinity or a NAN (not a number), where a <script> would
  stop with an error: 1/0 is INF, -1/0 is -INF, ln(0) is -INF and 0/0 is
  NAN(004). A NAN shows the code <HyperCard> gave it for what made it,
  as <HyperCard> showed it: sqrt(-1) is -NAN(001) and ln(-1) is
  NAN(036). An infinity or a NAN shows in capitals, where it shows as
  inf or nan otherwise. Text such as NAN(036) is read back as the NAN
  with that code, and a NAN is compared as text, so that x is "NAN(036)"
  is true when x is ln(-1), as <HyperCard> stacks that test for one
  expect: as a number, a NAN is equal to nothing. A number beyond this
  engine's range, such as 10^400, is INF too: <HyperCard|HyperCard's>
  numbers went further. <div> and <mod> by zero still stop the <script>,
  as they did in <HyperCard>.
- A <script> that reads the <textFont>, <textSize>, <textStyle> or
  <textHeight> of a <button> or a <field> with none of its own gets the
  one it uses, where it would get empty: in <HyperCard> every <button>
  and <field> had its own. The <textSize> of a <button> that takes its
  <stack|stack's> 12 point is 12. The <textFont>, <textSize> and
  <textStyle> of text in a <field> with none of its own are the
  <field|field's>. After a <script> sets the <textSize>, which sets the
  <textHeight> to none, the <textHeight> is the one for that size, as
  <HyperCard> set it. And the <effective> <textStyle> of an <object>
  with a style of its own but no <textSize> of its own is that style,
  the one it is drawn with, where otherwise it is its owner's. A
  <script> can set a <textStyle> to <HyperCard|HyperCard's> own style
  names, from a variable or a field too: condense and extend are
  condensed and expanded, and outline and shadow, which this engine
  does not draw, are left out, where they stop the <script> otherwise.
- The <round> <function> takes a number halfway between two integers to
  the even one, as <statRound> does and as <HyperCard|HyperCard's>
  round did: round(2.5) is 2, round(3.5) is 4 and round(-2.5) is -2,
  where otherwise they are 3, 4 and -3. With a precision, which
  <HyperCard|HyperCard's> round did not take, <round> is the same in
  every <stack>.

File > Import Stack keeps the <doMenu> <handler|handlers> of a <stack>
it imports with the <hyperTalkCompatible> true, and gives the <stack>
a substack, "HyperCard Menus" and the <stack|stack's> name, whose
<doMenu> <handler> chooses <HyperCard|HyperCard's> menu items (New
Card, Paste Card, Bring Closer...) for the menu items no other
<handler> takes. With HyperCard Message Order turned off, it renames
them hcDoMenu instead, and <doMenu> in the <stack|stack's>
<script|scripts> calls hcDoMenu.

The <stack> keeps the <property> as its <custom property> of the same
name, and is saved with it, so the <stack file> format does not change.
An engine without the <property> opens the <stack> with its usual
rules, under which no <handler> can be called <doMenu>: a <script>
with one does not compile there.

Set the <hyperTalkCompatible> before the <stack|stack's> <script|scripts>
are first compiled: a <script> already compiled keeps the <handler>
names it was compiled with until it is set again.

